                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "55da6439-35d0-442c-badd-c42d5b3d395b",
                        "entry_id": "5bff2e49-37dc-32b3-9f61-86950ab13e59",
                        "tags": [
                            "past"
                        ],
                        "form": "effigiated"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a383a0b8-127e-386b-af40-f8a0b933d0e4",
                        "entry_id": "5bff2e49-37dc-32b3-9f61-86950ab13e59",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To form as an effigy or make a representation of; to illustrate or depict."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2f9589c8-1328-45c1-bd21-d8a5a9c20548",
                                "sense_id": "a383a0b8-127e-386b-af40-f8a0b933d0e4",
                                "text": "Let me give you a hint and instance of it, in that one and remarkable example of Mary Magdalen, who though she were a notorious Malefactor, and a great and grievous Sinner, as the History Evangelical effigiates, and sets her forth unto us yet, hearing that Jesus sate down to meat in a Pharifee's house, she presumed and made bold to go into the house; and bringing an Alablaster-Box of Oyntment, she stood at his feet behind him weeping, and began to wash his feet with tears;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "10cc43e4-397c-497a-9901-4751bfdee333",
                                "sense_id": "a383a0b8-127e-386b-af40-f8a0b933d0e4",
                                "text": "These tables were first made by God himself, Exod. xxxi. 18, and given into the hands of Moses; and when they were broken he was commanded […] to effigiate them, or cut stones after their image, into their likeness, for the first were seen only by himself, Deut. x. 11; Exod. xxxiv. 1."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1bb42f18-b3f5-4d50-817f-f6251789d914",
                                "sense_id": "a383a0b8-127e-386b-af40-f8a0b933d0e4",
                                "text": "In other words, metaphysical wit discovers infinity in time, provides a transcendent vision of life and a means to truth, and effigiates, that is, portrays the secular in the sacred, the sacred in the secular."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4473c823-9f1d-45a7-8cf3-7a44fb5abf13",
                                "sense_id": "a383a0b8-127e-386b-af40-f8a0b933d0e4",
                                "text": "In the same way CAUSALITY is effigiated, not merely by cogitating a WORKER, but by representing such an actuating thing as antecedes in time, and upon which somewhat else invariably follows."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "81466236-261e-3c0a-b840-b92bec4bd8fa",
                        "entry_id": "5bff2e49-37dc-32b3-9f61-86950ab13e59",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To fashion; to adapt."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly",
                            "transitive"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "38719607-903d-4527-b135-3fb404385b93",
                                "sense_id": "81466236-261e-3c0a-b840-b92bec4bd8fa",
                                "text": "The Pattern we were effigiated to, was infinitely knowing, infinitely good; in both we are bound to imitate: bound to be the Reprefentatives of both, or we forfeit our Original, and our similitude, too."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4ea564c3-a8ca-4b6a-a41a-01175aaafc70",
                                "sense_id": "81466236-261e-3c0a-b840-b92bec4bd8fa",
                                "text": "He who means to win souls, must, as St. Paul did, effigiate and conform himself to those circumstances of living and discourse by which he may prevail."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3665bdbe-1c5f-4e4c-8aa6-7674d1d1554c",
                                "sense_id": "81466236-261e-3c0a-b840-b92bec4bd8fa",
                                "text": "A carnall mans designe, gives the stamp and impression to his Religion, and effigiates and mouldeth it; he will never make choice of such an one as shall be destructive to his ends."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2a05d204-b4f4-3b66-970a-984b93b68ce5",
                        "entry_id": "5bff2e49-37dc-32b3-9f61-86950ab13e59",
                        "glosses": [
                            "To embody; to manifest"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "da41f9c2-8c12-4295-87cd-879a4328e6ab",
                                "sense_id": "2a05d204-b4f4-3b66-970a-984b93b68ce5",
                                "text": "all this notwithstanding, they that are in their tombes & graves shall heare the voice of the Son of man, & earth, earth, earth in Jeremy winnowed and boulted by death into the smallest dust, shall be effigiated and fhaped anew into living men;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "1194e437-f3b4-4795-b51c-0e51df557236",
                                "sense_id": "2a05d204-b4f4-3b66-970a-984b93b68ce5",
                                "text": "Th'Effigies here th'engraver hath don it: But where's the man effigiates his wit?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e691d4a3-38c8-4fae-b236-2c769b9dc8df",
                                "sense_id": "2a05d204-b4f4-3b66-970a-984b93b68ce5",
                                "text": "He concludes by explaining the importance of having a body in order to understand its less knowable partner: \"I know that in the state of my body, which is more discernible, than that of my soule, thou dost effigiate my Soule to me ” ( 119 ) ."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9f1be1f3-73b9-4823-a67e-c162809b189d",
                                "sense_id": "2a05d204-b4f4-3b66-970a-984b93b68ce5",
                                "text": "In this context he states: \"Nor are there only such several Spirits of Trees, Herbs, Grass, and of every Species of them, which by a Proper Plastical Veritue Created in and with them by God do severaly Effigiate their Proper Bodys, and the Organism thereof, but also Proper Subordinate Vegetative Spirits of Fishes, Fowls, and Beasts, and of every Species of them, which doth so Effigiate their Proper Bodys , and the Organism therof.\""
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
